  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The graph of $y=-f(2x)+4,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translation by $4$ units up\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translation by $4$ units down\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-f(2x)+4,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Reflect $y=f(2x)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1):$ Translate $y=-f(2x)$ up by $4$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(2x)+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1).$",
